# Bring your own reverse proxy

The Docker Compose bundle ships with Caddy, which obtains and renews TLS
certificates for you. If you already run a reverse proxy -- nginx, Traefik,
HAProxy, your own Caddy, a homelab edge -- you can terminate TLS there instead
and forward plain HTTP to Nocturne's gateway.

Nocturne needs three request headers from your proxy to work. Everything else on
this page is ordinary reverse-proxy configuration; the headers are the part that
is easy to miss, and missing them produces the confusing failures listed under
Troubleshooting below.

## Start the stack without Caddy

The bundle includes an override that parks Caddy under an inactive profile and
publishes the gateway on host port `8080` as plain HTTP:

```bash
docker compose -f docker-compose.yaml -f docker-compose.byo-proxy.yaml up -d
```

Run `docker compose ps` afterwards and confirm no `caddy` container is listed. If
one is running it still holds host ports 80 and 443, and it will fight your proxy
for them.

<Callout type="warning" title="The gateway speaks plain HTTP only">
  Nothing behind port 8080 terminates TLS. Point your proxy at
  <code>http://&lt;host&gt;:8080</code>. A browser opened directly at
  <code>https://&lt;host&gt;:8080</code>, or a <code>proxy_pass https://</code>
  aimed at it, will always fail the TLS handshake.
</Callout>

## DNS and certificates

Nocturne is multi-tenant: the apex domain serves the marketing and login pages,
every tenant gets its own subdomain of `BASE_DOMAIN`, and public share links sit
one label deeper again, at `{token}.share.example.com`. Your proxy has to
terminate all three.

- `BASE_DOMAIN` in `.env` must be a hostname of **at least two labels**, and not
  an IP address. It does *not* have to be a registrable root domain --
  `nocturne.example.com` is fine, and tenants are then
  `tenant.nocturne.example.com`. An IP address is what to avoid: WebAuthn refuses
  an IP as a relying-party ID, so passkey login stops working, and tenant
  subdomains have nowhere to live.
- DNS needs an **A** record for the apex and a wildcard **A** record for
  `*.example.com`. A DNS wildcard answers at any depth, so that one record
  already covers `{token}.share.example.com` -- unless you have also created a
  real `share.example.com` record, which stops the wildcard applying below it.
- The certificate is the part that does *not* stretch. An X.509 wildcard matches
  exactly one label, so `*.example.com` covers tenants and nothing deeper: you
  need **both** `*.example.com` and `*.share.example.com` as SANs, or share links
  fail the handshake while tenants work. Wildcards require a **DNS-01**
  challenge, because HTTP-01 and TLS-ALPN-01 cannot issue them.

<Callout type="tip" title="Wildcards are not the only option">
  A proxy that mints certificates per hostname on first request needs no wildcard
  and no DNS credentials. That is what the bundled Caddy does, and you can do the
  same &mdash; with one constraint, described under Caddy below.
</Callout>

## The three headers

Nocturne's web container reconstructs the URL the browser is actually looking at
from `X-Forwarded-Proto` and `X-Forwarded-Host`, falling back to `Host`. It uses
that reconstructed origin to validate state-changing requests, so if it does not
match what the browser sees, every form submission is rejected.

The gateway passes these headers through untouched rather than inventing its own,
so whatever your proxy sends is what Nocturne sees. Your proxy must set all
three:

| Header | Value | Why |
|--------|-------|-----|
| `Host` | The original request host | Selects the tenant. Most proxies default to the *upstream* address instead, which is the single most common misconfiguration. |
| `X-Forwarded-Proto` | `https` | Without it the app believes the browser is on plain HTTP and rejects requests as cross-site. |
| `X-Forwarded-Host` | The original request host | Used ahead of `Host` when reconstructing the origin. |

<Callout type="danger" title="nginx does not send these by default">
  nginx's default is <code>proxy_set_header Host $proxy_host</code> -- the literal
  <code>proxy_pass</code> target, e.g. <code>192.168.1.10:8080</code> -- and it
  sends no <code>X-Forwarded-*</code> headers at all. A stock
  <code>proxy_pass</code> with no <code>proxy_set_header</code> lines gets you a
  login page that loads and a login attempt that 403s.
</Callout>

## nginx

```nginx
# Required for SignalR / WebSocket upgrades.
map $http_upgrade $connection_upgrade {
    default upgrade;
    ''      close;
}

server {
    listen 80;
    server_name example.com *.example.com;
    return 301 https://$host$request_uri;
}

server {
    listen 443 ssl;
    # nginx 1.25.1 and newer. On older builds -- Ubuntu 22.04, Debian 12 and
    # Ubuntu 24.04 all ship one -- this directive does not exist and nginx
    # refuses to start; use `listen 443 ssl http2;` above instead and drop it.
    http2 on;
    server_name example.com *.example.com;

    # Wildcard certificates, issued via DNS-01.
    ssl_certificate     /etc/letsencrypt/live/example.com/fullchain.pem;
    ssl_certificate_key /etc/letsencrypt/live/example.com/privkey.pem;

    # Large response headers overflow nginx's 4k default and surface as 502.
    proxy_buffer_size           16k;
    proxy_buffers            8  16k;
    proxy_busy_buffers_size     32k;
    large_client_header_buffers 4 16k;

    client_max_body_size 50m;

    location / {
        # Plain HTTP. The gateway does not terminate TLS.
        proxy_pass http://127.0.0.1:8080;

        # nginx defaults to HTTP/1.0, which breaks keepalive and upgrades.
        proxy_http_version 1.1;

        proxy_set_header Host              $host;
        proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-Host  $host;
        proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-Proto $scheme;
        proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-For   $proxy_add_x_forwarded_for;
        proxy_set_header X-Real-IP         $remote_addr;

        proxy_set_header Upgrade    $http_upgrade;
        proxy_set_header Connection $connection_upgrade;

        # Real-time updates stream; buffering stalls them.
        proxy_buffering    off;
        proxy_read_timeout 300s;
        proxy_send_timeout 300s;
    }
}
```

nginx's `*.example.com` matches at any depth, so the `server_name` above already
covers share hosts, as does the DNS wildcard. The certificate is the one that
does not stretch -- it still needs `*.share.example.com` as a second SAN.

If nginx listens on a non-standard port, use `$http_host` in place of `$host` for
both host headers so the port survives.

## Traefik

Traefik sets `X-Forwarded-*` automatically and preserves the original `Host`, so
a plain router works. Labels on the `gateway` service:

```yaml
labels:
  traefik.enable: "true"
  traefik.http.routers.nocturne.rule: "Host(`example.com`) || HostRegexp(`^.+[.]example[.]com$`)"
  traefik.http.routers.nocturne.entrypoints: "websecure"
  traefik.http.routers.nocturne.tls.certresolver: "dns"
  traefik.http.routers.nocturne.tls.domains[0].main: "example.com"
  traefik.http.routers.nocturne.tls.domains[0].sans: "*.example.com,*.share.example.com"
  traefik.http.services.nocturne.loadbalancer.server.port: "5000"
```

The dots use the character class `[.]`, which matches a literal dot without
needing a backslash escape.

`HostRegexp` takes a standard Go regular expression on Traefik v3. Traefik v2's
named-group form is not an *error* on v3 -- the router loads and reports no
error, it just silently matches nothing, so every tenant subdomain 404s while the
apex keeps working:

```yaml
# Traefik v2 syntax. On v3 this matches the apex and nothing else.
traefik.http.routers.nocturne.rule: "Host(`example.com`) || HostRegexp(`{sub:.+}.example.com`)"
```

Use that older form only on Traefik v2. If you are on v3 and want to keep it, add
`traefik.http.routers.nocturne.rulesyntax: "v2"` so v3 parses the rule the old
way.

The v3 pattern matches at any depth, so it covers share hosts; the certificate is
what needs the second SAN. The resolver must be configured for DNS-01, or neither
wildcard will issue.

Traefik reads these labels over the Docker socket but connects to the container
itself, so it has to be attached to the bundle's `aspire` network to reach the
gateway on port 5000. Once it is, it no longer needs the override's published
port at all -- and dropping `ports` from the override, rather than binding it to
loopback, is the safest version of the warning below.

## Caddy

If you run your own Caddy rather than the bundled one, it sets the headers for
you. A Caddy site-address wildcard matches one label, like an X.509 wildcard and
unlike DNS, so the share hosts have to be named separately:

```caddyfile
example.com, *.example.com, *.share.example.com {
    tls {
        dns <your-provider> {env.PROVIDER_API_TOKEN}
    }
    reverse_proxy http://127.0.0.1:8080
}
```

That is the wildcard route, and it needs a DNS provider token. The bundled Caddy
takes the other one: it issues a certificate per hostname on first request, with
no wildcard and no DNS credentials, and asks the API whether a hostname is a real
tenant before doing so.

Reproducing that in your own Caddy has one constraint. The `ask` endpoint answers
200 or 404 for a given hostname, which makes it an anonymous tenant-slug oracle,
so the web container deliberately refuses it on the `/api` path the gateway
serves. Caddy must therefore reach the API container directly rather than through
the gateway on port 8080 -- which in practice means running Caddy as a service on
the bundle's compose network:

```caddyfile
{
    on_demand_tls {
        ask http://nocturne-api:8080/api/v4/platform/tls-authorize
    }
}

example.com {
    reverse_proxy gateway:5000
}

*.example.com {
    tls {
        on_demand
    }
    reverse_proxy gateway:5000
}
```

Do not point `ask` at a host that is reachable from the internet, and do not drop
it: without it, anyone who points DNS at your server can drive certificate
issuance for arbitrary names.

<Callout type="warning" title="On-demand does not cover share links yet">
  The <code>ask</code> endpoint authorizes the apex and active tenant subdomains
  only; it answers 404 for a <code>&#123;token&#125;.share.example.com</code> host, so Caddy
  declines to issue and the share link fails the handshake. Until that is fixed,
  a deployment that hands out share links needs the wildcard route above, with
  <code>*.share.example.com</code> among its SANs.
</Callout>

## Client addresses and the exposed port

Audit rows and rate-limit buckets record the caller's address, which Nocturne takes
from the **rightmost** `X-Forwarded-For` entry. A proxy that appends is therefore
safe: nginx's `$proxy_add_x_forwarded_for`, and Caddy and Traefik by default, put
the address they actually saw last, and anything a caller prepended is never read.

Behind a CDN, keep that chain and raise the hop count instead of collapsing it, or
the address recorded is your CDN's rather than the client's. Set
`ForwardedHeaders__ForwardLimit` on `nocturne-api` to the number of hops you trust,
and optionally `ForwardedHeaders__KnownProxies` or `ForwardedHeaders__KnownNetworks`
to pin which addresses may speak for a client.

Those two are not independent. If you raise `ForwardLimit` *and* declare a
known-proxy list, every intermediate hop must appear in the list: the walk stops
at the first address that is not on it, and the entries beyond it are silently
left unread. Declare all of them, or none.

<Callout type="warning" title="Do not leave port 8080 open to the world">
  A caller that reaches the gateway directly, bypassing your proxy, does get to
  choose its own <code>X-Forwarded-For</code>. Publish the port on the interface
  your proxy uses rather than all of them &mdash; in the override, change
  <code>"8080:5000"</code> to <code>"127.0.0.1:8080:5000"</code> when the proxy runs
  on the same host.
</Callout>

## Troubleshooting

| Symptom | Cause |
|---------|-------|
| Login, or anything that writes, returns **403** with `Cross-site remote requests are forbidden` | `X-Forwarded-Proto` and `X-Forwarded-Host` are missing or wrong, so the reconstructed origin does not match the browser's. A client posting a form content type sees `Cross-site POST form submissions are forbidden` instead; same cause. |
| The page shows **HTTPS Required** | You reached the site over plain HTTP on something other than `localhost`. Passkey registration needs a secure context, so finish the TLS setup before running first-run setup. |
| **SSL error** connecting to port 8080 | Something is speaking TLS to the gateway. It serves plain HTTP only. |
| **502 Bad Gateway** | Check your proxy's error log; it names the cause. See below. |
| Tenant subdomains 404 or fail TLS while the apex works | No wildcard DNS record, or a certificate that does not cover `*.example.com`. |
| Tenant subdomains work but **share links** fail TLS | The certificate covers `*.example.com` only, which matches one label. Add `*.share.example.com` as a second SAN. The DNS wildcard already answers at that depth. |

For a 502, nginx's `/var/log/nginx/error.log` is decisive:

| error.log says | Cause |
|----------------|-------|
| `connect() failed (111: Connection refused)` | The gateway is not reachable at that address. Confirm the override is applied and port 8080 is published. |
| `SSL_do_handshake() failed` | `proxy_pass https://` pointed at the plain-HTTP gateway. |
| `upstream sent too big header` | Raise `proxy_buffer_size` as shown above. |
| `upstream prematurely closed connection` | Add `proxy_http_version 1.1`. |

<Callout type="tip" title="Confirming the headers arrive">
  <code>docker compose logs gateway</code> records the host each request arrived
  on. If that shows your upstream address rather than your domain, your proxy is
  not forwarding <code>Host</code> -- fix that before looking anywhere else.
</Callout>

## Next steps

Once the site loads over HTTPS on your domain, continue with
[first-run setup](/docs/getting-started).
